 Amish Shah, keep the race competitive", "Public polling is nearly nonexistent; the only poll is a year-old Democratic-sponsored hypothetical showing a 45-46 tie"], "rationale": "The district's slight Republican lean is outweighed by a favorable national environment, and six of ten expert ratings call it Lean Democratic while markets price Democratic chances near 80 percent. Feely's cash edge and the heavy outside spending against Shah argue for a competitive rather than safe Democratic position.", "watch": "Fresh polling or the cumulative effect of Republican outside attacks on Shah could tighten this into a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: David Schweikert (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Arizona's 1st District is an open seat after Republican David Schweikert left to run for governor, and it sits at the center of the fight for House control. The district leans slightly Republican on partisanship, but the 2026 national environment favors Democrats, and Amish Shah enters with strong fundraising. Jay Feely, a former NFL kicker and broadcaster, has actually out-raised Shah and holds a cash advantage.\n\nTen expert ratings split six for Lean Democratic, three for Likely Democratic and one Tossup, and prediction markets put Democratic chances near 80 percent. Public polling is nearly nonexistent — a single Democratic-sponsored poll from November 2025 showed a 45-46 tie. Republican outside groups have poured about $2.7 million into attacks on Shah, while Feely has faced scrutiny over past comments about female athletes. The race stands as a Lean Democratic contest with wide uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7982, "margin": 5.27}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7812, "margin": 5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7872, "margin": 5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7849, "margin": 5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p